  "body": "## What changed\n\n- `contour_comp::door` (`crates/motion/src/machining2d/contour_comp.rs`) refuses a 2D Contour under control or wear compensation when its lead-in or its lead-out is `Linear`. The refusal names the mode and the end, for example: \"control compensation does not ride a Linear lead-in - the entry's descent at its start runs on the ring's own offset before G41/G42 engages, so a register over the nominal radius, or a positive wear value, cuts the finished wall there; pick the Perpendicular lead-in, or Computer compensation\".\n- The rule covers both ends: the mirrored lead-out has the same defect at its end, because the cancel withdrawal lands on the ring line past G40.\n- The straight-lead refusal no longer offers the Linear shape, and the approach page's consequence line says the same (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/approach_page.rs`).\n- The existing comp fixtures moved from the Linear lead to the Perpendicular lead; the four control-comp and four wear-comp G-code goldens were regenerated. The dive point in each golden moves from `(11, -3)` - one nominal radius off the part, the defect - to `(5, -9)`, the radius plus the lead. The compensated ring itself did not move.\n- Decision: `docs/decisions/3891-comp-entry-wall.md`. Refusal over proof: no law bounds the operator's register, and a Linear lead's dive point stands ON the ring's offset line, so no register over the nominal (plus the stock) is safe there. Refusal over an engage reorder: RS-274 / ISO 6983 builds the offset over a straight XY block, so an axial plunge or a vertical-plane arc cannot carry G41/G42. Refusal over a moved dive point: a new lead shape with a new room-law proof.\n\nBuilds on the Ramp/Helix refusal of #3890, now in `main`: this branch carries that commit as its base, and its fixtures now use the Perpendicular lead (its pins still pass).\n\n## The geometry reading\n\n- Between the end of the rapid and the first move with G41/G42 active, the Plunge entry posts one axial dive at the lead-in's start, and the Arc entry posts a vertical-plane arc whose XY footprint is the straight chord onto that same point (`entry_arc`).\n- A `Linear` lead starts ON the ring's own offset line: one nominal radius plus the stock off the finished wall. The machine runs those moves at the nominal centre under G40, so a register over the nominal radius, or a positive wear value over the stock, cuts the finished wall at the dive point (and at the cancel's end) before G41/G42 engages. The old golden showed it: the dive at `(11, -3)` against the 10 x 10 part, 3 mm off the wall.\n- A `Perpendicular` lead starts its length further into the air. The door's length law (at least the tool radius, re-checked on the actual move by `push_span`) holds that at one full radius, so every pre-engage position keeps at least (radius + lead length) off the finished wall. That is the maximum register difference the door vouches for: the register must sit one full radius over the nominal before the wall is at risk.\n- The bore side reads the same: under compensation the plunge's `disc_clears` test always takes the lead start (the door guarantees the lead length), so the bore's dive and the bore-side arc chord stand the same clearance inside the bore.\n- The Ramp and Helix entries and level transitions were already refused by #3890; their cases are covered, on both sides, and this PR's pins confirm those refusals still fire with the Perpendicular fixture.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- `crates/motion/tests/machining2d.rs` `a_linear_lead_under_control_or_wear_is_refused_by_name_on_both_sides`: lead-in and lead-out, Control and Wear, Side::Outside and Side::Inside; the same parameters in Computer generate.\n- `crates/motion/tests/machining2d.rs` `the_perpendicular_comp_entry_keeps_the_lead_length_beyond_the_ring`: Control and Wear, both sides, Plunge and Arc entry - every cut-adjacent move before each span's engage stands at least the radius (5) plus the lead (6) off the part, and the closest position is the dive point itself. The bore side is pinned here because a whole-model source posts only the wall side.\n- `crates/app/tests/control_comp.rs` `a_linear_lead_in_or_out_under_control_or_wear_is_refused_and_posts_nothing`: through `GenerateOperation`; no toolpath is stored and the post gate writes no G41/G42 program.\n- `crates/app/tests/control_comp.rs` `the_entry_descents_before_g41_hold_the_wall_clear_under_an_oversized_register_and_positive_wear`: reads the posted Fanuc program. An oversized register (5.0 mm on the D6 tool, 2 mm over its nominal 3) and a positive wear value (1.0 mm, effective radius 4.0) do not touch the finished wall at any stand under G40; the closest stand is the dive point at 9 mm, the radius plus the lead. Plunge and Arc entries, Control and Wear.\n\n## What was not exercised\n\n- A posted bore-side program: no whole-model fixture in the app tests yields an inside ring, so the bore side is pinned on the CL positions (the exact moves the post writes under G40) at the motion level.\n- The stands are checked as positions, not swept segments: the fixture's under-G40 segments are vertical plunges at a fixed XY and one diagonal rapid at clearance height, so the endpoints cover them.\n- The register values are modeled in the test (the machine's D register is not part of the program text); the CL carries the nominal radius only, by design.\n\n## Gate\n\n- The mapped suites, run by hand at `-j 8`: `camcraft-motion` lib (619 passed, 22 ignored - heavy lane), `climb_direction` (9), `contour_start_comp` (5), `control_comp` (8), `machining2d` whole suite (75), `wear_comp` (2); `camcraft-app` lib (306) and the touched suites `control_comp` (9), `wear_comp` (5); `camcraft-ui` lib (1588 passed, 8 ignored - snapshot lane) and the eight suites the gate map names for `ui::egui_app::approach_page` (boundary 3, boundary_picks 7, contour_dxf_sketch 2, sinker_setup 2, tap_hand 3, theme_example 3, theme_files 17, waterjet_tool 1). Clippy `-p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- The full `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` could not complete on this machine. Its plan falls back to the whole `camcraft-app` and `camcraft-ui` suite sets (766 steps) because the G-code goldens under `tests/golden/` count as shared test inputs - that fallback builds every app test binary, about 90 GB, and the link failed at 1.1 GB free (`LNK1180`, `LNK1318`). The goldens this PR changes are read by exactly the two app suites above, which ran green twice (with and without `WRITE_GOLDEN=1`).\n- sccache's wrapper crashed rustc (`STATUS_STACK_BUFFER_OVERRUN`) at this machine's full parallelism early in the session; every build after that used no wrapper and a lowered job budget.\n\nChangelog: 2D Contour under control or wear compensation refuses a Linear lead-in or lead-out by name - its dive point runs on the ring's own offset, where a register over the nominal radius cuts the finished wall before G41/G42 engages; the Perpendicular lead stays legal and its clearance is pinned from the posted G-code.\n\nEffort: high\n\nFixes #3891\n\n## Screenshots\n\nBefore, parent `6dc848512`. The consequence line still offers a Linear lead:\n\n![Before: Approach page consequence line with In control selected](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FlamingFireCoding/Camcraft/pr-screenshots/pr3912-approach-consequence-before.png)\n\nAfter, head `5dbe2228d`. The line names the Linear lead as refused:\n\n![After: Approach page consequence line with In control selected](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FlamingFireCoding/Camcraft/pr-screenshots/pr3912-approach-consequence-after.png)",
